		},
		{
			"id": "K1N432Z1",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -382.7636052080014,
			"y": 3062.4590333777364,
			"width": 1066.998779296875,
			"height": 375,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b2U",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1567521278,
			"version": 491,
			"versionNonce": 1692165950,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783611765039,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "1. Left Child: 2i + 1\n  - Why? Going down a level doubles the number of nodes. Multiplying by 2 skips all the nodes \n    on the current level, and +1 lands on the left child.\n  - Example: Node B is at index 1.Left child is 2(1) + 1 = 3 (Node D).\n2. Right Child: 2i + 2\n  - Why? The right child sits immediately next to the left child (+1 slot over).\n  - Example: Right child is 2(1) + 2 = 4 (Node E).\n3. Parent: (i - 1) / 2\n  - Why? Inverting the left-child formula. Thanks to integer division in languages like Java, \n    decimal values are automatically floored, mapping both left and right siblings back to their \n    exact parent index.\n  - Example:\n      a. If you are the left child (Index 3): (3 - 1)/2 = 2/2 = 1. (Parent is at index 1).\n      b. If you are the right child (Index 4): (4 - 1)/2 = 3/2 = 1.5. Drop the .5, and you get 1!\n      c. Both siblings perfectly point back to the exact same parent!",
			"rawText": "1. Left Child: 2i + 1\n  - Why? Going down a level doubles the number of nodes. Multiplying by 2 skips all the nodes \n    on the current level, and +1 lands on the left child.\n  - Example: Node B is at index 1.Left child is 2(1) + 1 = 3 (Node D).\n2. Right Child: 2i + 2\n  - Why? The right child sits immediately next to the left child (+1 slot over).\n  - Example: Right child is 2(1) + 2 = 4 (Node E).\n3. Parent: (i - 1) / 2\n  - Why? Inverting the left-child formula. Thanks to integer division in languages like Java, \n    decimal values are automatically floored, mapping both left and right siblings back to their \n    exact parent index.\n  - Example:\n      a. If you are the left child (Index 3): (3 - 1)/2 = 2/2 = 1. (Parent is at index 1).\n      b. If you are the right child (Index 4): (4 - 1)/2 = 3/2 = 1.5. Drop the .5, and you get 1!\n      c. Both siblings perfectly point back to the exact same parent!",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": "dR9hcCsB",
			"originalText": "1. Left Child: 2i + 1\n  - Why? Going down a level doubles the number of nodes. Multiplying by 2 skips all the nodes \n    on the current level, and +1 lands on the left child.\n  - Example: Node B is at index 1.Left child is 2(1) + 1 = 3 (Node D).\n2. Right Child: 2i + 2\n  - Why? The right child sits immediately next to the left child (+1 slot over).\n  - Example: Right child is 2(1) + 2 = 4 (Node E).\n3. Parent: (i - 1) / 2\n  - Why? Inverting the left-child formula. Thanks to integer division in languages like Java, \n    decimal values are automatically floored, mapping both left and right siblings back to their \n    exact parent index.\n  - Example:\n      a. If you are the left child (Index 3): (3 - 1)/2 = 2/2 = 1. (Parent is at index 1).\n      b. If you are the right child (Index 4): (4 - 1)/2 = 3/2 = 1.5. Drop the .5, and you get 1!\n      c. Both siblings perfectly point back to the exact same parent!",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
